.TH SYS_SLEEP 2
.SH NAME
sys_sleep, sys_wake \- sleep, and end another thread's sleep (SYS_SLEEP, SYS_WAKE)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_sleep(uint64_t ns);
long sys_wake(int tid);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_sleep
puts the calling thread to sleep for at least
.I ns
nanoseconds. The timer is tickless: the thread is woken when its time
comes, not at the next tick. 0 just lets other threads run, as
.BR sys_yield (2).
.PP
.B sys_wake
ends the sleep of thread
.I tid
of the same process early: its
.B sys_sleep
returns 1. If that thread is not asleep, its next sleep ends at once: a
wake-up is never lost.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR sys_sleep :
0 when the time has passed, 1 when another thread woke it, or a negative
error.
.BR sys_wake :
0, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINTR
.RB ( sys_sleep )
A signal the process catches came.
.TP
.B EPIPE
.RB ( sys_sleep )
The process is ending.
.TP
.B ESRCH
.RB ( sys_wake )
No thread
.I tid
in this process.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_clock (2),
.BR sys_futex (2),
.BR sleep (1)
